1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,609
Someone on your team sends you that
message, with a screenshot of a terminal.

2
00:00:08,649 --> 00:00:11,464
They're a little stressed,
and they want one command.

3
00:00:11,804 --> 00:00:15,118
You now know five undo commands
and a dozen forms of them.

4
00:00:15,158 --> 00:00:18,413
The risk is that you answer
with the first one that fits.

5
00:00:18,453 --> 00:00:23,270
A senior answer doesn't start with a command.
It starts with a question: "Has anyone

6
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:26,854
else got that commit?" Then one
command, with its risk stated.

7
00:00:26,894 --> 00:00:31,479
Hold on to that question. By scenario
seven it should come out before you think.

8
00:00:31,819 --> 00:00:35,041
This video trains exactly
that reflex, seven times.

9
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:40,627
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:40,667 --> 00:00:43,792
This is the consolidation
video of the undo module.

11
00:00:43,832 --> 00:00:48,632
There's no new mechanism in it. Everything
was taught in the last five videos:

12
00:00:48,672 --> 00:00:53,597
restore, reset and its modes, amend,
revert, reverting a merge, clean and stash.

13
00:00:53,937 --> 00:00:58,578
What is new is that it all goes onto
one page. One table that says, for each

14
00:00:58,618 --> 00:01:03,691
command, what happens to HEAD, the branch,
the index, the working tree and history.

15
00:01:03,731 --> 00:01:08,249
One decision tree that leads from "what
do you want to undo" to a command.

16
00:01:08,289 --> 00:01:13,300
And then seven situations, in a clone whose
origin is a bare repository on disk, a

17
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:16,627
repository with no working
tree that plays the server.

18
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:19,337
So "pushed" is a real
state and not a story.

19
00:01:19,677 --> 00:01:24,581
The format for the scenarios: you see the
situation, you choose, and only then you

20
00:01:24,621 --> 00:01:28,740
see the command. Pause the video
each time. Choosing is the exercise.

21
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,993
After this video you can:

22
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:37,377
Choose the lowest-risk undo for a described
situation, and state its effect on HEAD,

23
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:40,525
the branch, the index, the
working tree and history.

24
00:01:40,865 --> 00:01:43,511
Justify the choice by whether
the history is shared.

25
00:01:43,851 --> 00:01:47,589
Carry out seven standard scenarios
and verify the end state.

26
00:01:47,929 --> 00:01:50,758
Name the choice that would
have been wrong, and why.

27
00:01:55,598 --> 00:01:59,809
In this table, "HEAD" is the commit that
HEAD resolves to, "Branch" is the ref of

28
00:01:59,849 --> 00:02:04,165
the current branch, and "History" is the
set of commits reachable from that branch.

29
00:02:04,505 --> 00:02:08,506
Don't try to memorize twelve rows.
Learn how to read the table.

30
00:02:08,846 --> 00:02:12,132
Read the Branch column first.
There are three words in it.

31
00:02:12,172 --> 00:02:15,861
"Set to" means the branch can lose
commits: private history only.

32
00:02:15,901 --> 00:02:19,933
"Advanced" and "unchanged" are safe
on a branch that other people have.

33
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:24,525
Then read the Working tree column for the
word "destroyed" and the phrase "from the

34
00:02:24,565 --> 00:02:28,868
index" or "from a commit". Those are the
rows where content that no object holds can

35
00:02:28,908 --> 00:02:29,644
be overwritten.

36
00:02:29,984 --> 00:02:34,119
Two columns, two questions. Is the
branch allowed to lose commits?

37
00:02:34,159 --> 00:02:36,459
Can I afford to lose what is on disk?

38
00:02:36,799 --> 00:02:42,058
Try it now, thirty seconds. Find the row
for git revert, and read its Branch cell

39
00:02:42,098 --> 00:02:45,852
out loud. Safe on a branch that
other people have, or not?

40
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:55,292
"Advanced by one". So yes, it's
safe: the branch only moves forward.

41
00:02:55,632 --> 00:03:00,593
Now the decision tree, which is today's
diagram. It asks three questions in order.

42
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:04,131
What do you want to undo:
uncommitted changes, or commits?

43
00:03:04,171 --> 00:03:09,132
If commits: can anyone else already have
them? And then: which of them, and do you

44
00:03:09,172 --> 00:03:10,657
want to keep the changes?

45
00:03:10,997 --> 00:03:16,151
Notice where the publication question sits.
It isn't asked for uncommitted changes,

46
00:03:16,191 --> 00:03:21,345
because nobody else can have those. It's
the first thing asked for commits, and the

47
00:03:21,385 --> 00:03:26,539
tree tells you how to answer it: git fetch,
then git branch -r --contains <commit>.

48
00:03:33,379 --> 00:03:37,470
A picture helps. Think of a triage
nurse. Triage isn't treatment.

49
00:03:37,510 --> 00:03:42,745
It's two or three questions, always the
same, always in the same order, that sort a

50
00:03:42,785 --> 00:03:47,956
case into the right room. The treatment in
each room is something you already know

51
00:03:47,996 --> 00:03:48,592
how to do.

52
00:03:48,932 --> 00:03:54,028
The decision tree is triage for undo. Where
it breaks as a picture: a nurse can look

53
00:03:54,068 --> 00:03:58,431
at the patient, while you often can't
see the state from a chat message.

54
00:03:58,471 --> 00:04:03,507
So before the tree, you need the facts. git
status -sb for what is uncommitted, and

55
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:08,398
whether you're ahead. A fetch, and git
branch -r --contains, for what is shared.

56
00:04:08,738 --> 00:04:13,213
And keep one more habit from the whole
module: say the risk out loud with the

57
00:04:13,253 --> 00:04:17,553
command. "This one has no way back."
"This one keeps your changes staged."

58
00:04:17,593 --> 00:04:21,774
"This one needs no force." A command
without its risk is half an answer.

59
00:04:23,614 --> 00:04:28,147
Read the tree top-down. The root question
has two branches: changes that aren't

60
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:29,478
committed, and commits.

61
00:04:29,818 --> 00:04:33,881
Under uncommitted changes there are five
leaves, and three of them are marked "no

62
00:04:33,921 --> 00:04:34,388
way back".

63
00:04:34,728 --> 00:04:37,854
Under commits, the publication
question comes first.

64
00:04:37,894 --> 00:04:42,360
NO leads to the commands that rewrite.
YES leads to the commands that add.

65
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:50,282
Scenario 1. You made a commit a minute ago,
and you want it back as staged changes.

66
00:04:50,322 --> 00:04:55,814
git status -sb says [ahead 1]. Which
branch of the tree, and which command?

67
00:04:55,854 --> 00:04:57,806
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

68
00:05:02,646 --> 00:05:08,724
ahead 1, and git branch -r --contains HEAD
prints nothing: no remote-tracking branch

69
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:14,842
contains the commit. Private history. git
reset --soft HEAD~1 🟡 takes the commit off

70
00:05:14,882 --> 00:05:17,755
the branch and leaves its change staged.

71
00:05:18,095 --> 00:05:23,349
The variations: git reset HEAD~1 to get
the change back unstaged, and git reset

72
00:05:23,389 --> 00:05:28,241
--keep HEAD~1 to drop it. The wrong
choice here would have been a revert.

73
00:05:28,281 --> 00:05:33,535
It works, and it leaves two commits of
noise in a history nobody else has seen.

74
00:05:33,875 --> 00:05:37,812
Scenario 2. A commit that
disabled retries is on main.

75
00:05:37,852 --> 00:05:38,328
Choose.

76
00:05:40,668 --> 00:05:45,379
git branch -r --contains c6f2cfd
prints origin/main: shared history.

77
00:05:45,419 --> 00:05:49,850
git revert 🟡, then an ordinary
push, accepted as a fast-forward.

78
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:55,719
The wrong choice is a reset and a forced
push, and the interview question at the end

79
00:05:55,759 --> 00:05:58,234
asks you to say why in three places.

80
00:05:58,574 --> 00:06:04,218
The same thing as a graph. The revert adds
one commit, 20cf7b0, and the push moves

81
00:06:04,258 --> 00:06:07,753
origin/main forward to it.
No label went backwards.

82
00:06:08,093 --> 00:06:12,843
Scenario 3. A .env file went in with
git add .. The commit isn't pushed.

83
00:06:12,883 --> 00:06:17,100
You want the commit without the
file, and the file kept on disk.

84
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:17,565
Choose.

85
00:06:19,905 --> 00:06:26,188
git rm --cached removes the index entry and
keeps the file, and git commit --amend 🟡

86
00:06:26,228 --> 00:06:32,360
replaces the commit: ccecf13 becomes 90e7514,
with one file in its stat instead of

87
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,177
two. The file is now untracked,
so it goes into .gitignore next.

88
00:06:37,517 --> 00:06:42,529
Two cautions from the textbook. The old
commit ccecf13 still contains the file and

89
00:06:42,569 --> 00:06:47,581
stays in your reflog, which is harmless
for a commit that never left your machine.

90
00:06:47,621 --> 00:06:52,633
And if a commit with a real secret was ever
pushed, amending isn't the fix: rotate

91
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:53,310
the secret.

92
00:06:53,650 --> 00:06:59,566
Scenario 4. rank.py is staged and
shouldn't be part of the next commit.

93
00:06:59,606 --> 00:07:04,347
The edit should stay. Quick
quiz. A, git restore rank.py.

94
00:07:04,387 --> 00:07:08,793
B, git restore --staged
rank.py. C, git reset --hard.

95
00:07:08,833 --> 00:07:09,799
Your answer?

96
00:07:14,639 --> 00:07:20,019
B. git restore --staged 🟡. The index entry
goes back to HEAD's version and the file

97
00:07:20,059 --> 00:07:24,654
keeps the edit: the M moves from the
first status column to the second.

98
00:07:24,694 --> 00:07:30,139
The wrong choice is git restore rank.py
without --staged, which would have discarded

99
00:07:30,179 --> 00:07:34,906
nothing here but, with further unstaged
edits on top, would destroy them.

100
00:07:35,246 --> 00:07:40,591
Scenario 5. You want to discard all local
changes: two edited tracked files and an

101
00:07:40,631 --> 00:07:43,743
untracked output file.
Choose, and say the risk.

102
00:07:46,083 --> 00:07:51,840
Tracked files: read git diff, then git
restore 🔴. Untracked files: git clean -n,

103
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:57,856
then -f 🔴. Neither step can be taken back,
and the five answers for both were given

104
00:07:57,896 --> 00:08:03,726
in their own videos. When in doubt, git
stash push -u does the same job and keeps

105
00:08:03,766 --> 00:08:04,595
the content.

106
00:08:04,935 --> 00:08:08,532
Scenario 6. You merged
feature/reranker a moment ago.

107
00:08:08,572 --> 00:08:14,226
It isn't pushed, and it was a mistake. You
also have an unrelated, uncommitted edit

108
00:08:14,266 --> 00:08:17,314
to metrics.txt that you
want to keep. Choose.

109
00:08:19,654 --> 00:08:25,508
git reset --merge ORIG_HEAD 🔴, dangerous
through staged work only, puts the branch

110
00:08:25,548 --> 00:08:31,402
back on the commit before the merge and
keeps the edit to metrics.txt: status goes

111
00:08:31,442 --> 00:08:35,356
from [ahead 2] to level, and
the M line is still there.

112
00:08:35,396 --> 00:08:41,035
If anything has overwritten ORIG_HEAD
since the merge, take the commit from the

113
00:08:41,075 --> 00:08:46,714
reflog. The wrong choice is --hard, which
would have taken metrics.txt with it.

114
00:08:47,054 --> 00:08:51,937
Scenario 7. The same merge, but pushed.
Choose, and say what else you have to do

115
00:08:51,977 --> 00:08:53,660
besides running the command.

116
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:01,545
origin/main contains the merge: shared
history. git revert -m 1 🟡, push, and write

117
00:09:01,585 --> 00:09:07,062
the re-merge plan of section 11.9 into the
ticket. The command takes ten seconds.

118
00:09:07,102 --> 00:09:10,263
The plan is what prevents
next week's incident.

119
00:09:12,103 --> 00:09:13,751
Five mistakes to watch for.

120
00:09:14,091 --> 00:09:18,521
Answering "how do I undo this" with a
command before asking about publication.

121
00:09:18,561 --> 00:09:23,106
Root cause: the same intent maps to a
rewrite on private history and to an added

122
00:09:23,146 --> 00:09:26,716
commit on shared history; the
commands are not interchangeable.

123
00:09:27,056 --> 00:09:32,430
Checking publication against a stale clone.
Root cause: git branch -r --contains

124
00:09:32,470 --> 00:09:36,964
reads remote-tracking branches,
which are as old as the last fetch.

125
00:09:37,304 --> 00:09:42,008
Using git reset --hard where
--keep or --merge ORIG_HEAD fits.

126
00:09:42,048 --> 00:09:47,745
Root cause: --hard rewrites the working
tree unconditionally, and unrelated

127
00:09:47,785 --> 00:09:50,270
uncommitted edits have no object.

128
00:09:50,610 --> 00:09:54,255
Amending a commit to remove a
secret that was already pushed.

129
00:09:54,295 --> 00:09:59,208
Root cause: the old commit still exists on
the server and in clones; the secret is

130
00:09:59,248 --> 00:10:01,021
exposed and has to be rotated.

131
00:10:01,361 --> 00:10:05,796
Reverting a pushed merge and stopping
there. Root cause: the merge stays in the

132
00:10:05,836 --> 00:10:10,498
graph, so the next merge of that branch
brings only newer commits unless a re-merge

133
00:10:10,538 --> 00:10:11,744
procedure is followed.

134
00:10:13,584 --> 00:10:19,031
Now, out of the lab. A platform team for
model serving keeps the decision tree in

135
00:10:19,071 --> 00:10:24,653
its on-call runbook, on the page titled
"Undo". Above the tree are three lines: run

136
00:10:24,693 --> 00:10:30,072
git status -sb, run git fetch, and run
git branch -r --contains on the commit in

137
00:10:30,112 --> 00:10:35,017
question. Below it, one rule in bold:
on main, the Branch column must say

138
00:10:35,057 --> 00:10:35,762
"advanced".

139
00:10:36,102 --> 00:10:39,890
During an incident the on-call
engineer isn't asked to remember Git.

140
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:44,225
They're asked to follow three questions.
The retrospective afterwards doesn't

141
00:10:44,265 --> 00:10:48,784
discuss which command was typed. It discusses
whether the questions were answered

142
00:10:48,824 --> 00:10:52,838
from evidence. That's a team that has
turned this module into a control.

143
00:10:54,678 --> 00:11:01,573
Your turn. Do Lab 8.7, "Scenario
cards", in lab-manual/m08-undo.md.

144
00:11:01,913 --> 00:11:05,526
For each card, before you touch
the keyboard, write three lines:

145
00:11:05,866 --> 00:11:08,623
Private or shared, and the
command output that proves it.

146
00:11:08,963 --> 00:11:13,484
The command you choose, and its row of the
table: HEAD, branch, index, working tree,

147
00:11:13,524 --> 00:11:13,918
history.

148
00:11:14,258 --> 00:11:17,480
The command that would have been
wrong, and what it would have cost.

149
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:20,537
Then run your choice and
verify the end state.

150
00:11:20,877 --> 00:11:27,350
The challenge is Exercise 8.9, Level 4,
"one bad commit that is public, one mixed

151
00:11:27,390 --> 00:11:31,934
commit that is not", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

152
00:11:33,774 --> 00:11:37,425
Q181: "A bad commit is on main
and the team has pulled it.

153
00:11:37,465 --> 00:11:42,453
Compare "revert and push" with "reset and
force-push": what does each do to the

154
00:11:42,493 --> 00:11:45,253
server, to the teammates'
clones and to CI?"

155
00:11:47,593 --> 00:11:52,231
Answer out loud first. The question gives
you its own structure: two procedures,

156
00:11:52,271 --> 00:11:56,968
three places. A strong answer fills all
six cells and uses the same vocabulary in

157
00:11:57,008 --> 00:12:01,646
each: which refs move, in which direction,
and what the next ordinary command in

158
00:12:01,686 --> 00:12:06,382
that place will then do. It should include
what happens when a teammate who still

159
00:12:06,422 --> 00:12:11,177
has the old commits pushes next. It closes
with what a server-side rule would have

160
00:12:11,217 --> 00:12:12,873
said to the second procedure.

161
00:12:14,713 --> 00:12:17,640
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

162
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:22,987
Before I undo a commit I fetch and check
whether a remote-tracking branch contains

163
00:12:23,027 --> 00:12:27,971
it. In the table I read the Branch column
first: "set to" is for private history,

164
00:12:28,011 --> 00:12:31,356
"advanced" and "unchanged"
are safe on shared branches.

165
00:12:31,396 --> 00:12:36,341
Uncommitted changes go through restore,
stash or clean, and three of those leaves

166
00:12:36,381 --> 00:12:41,326
have no way back. Private commits are
rewritten with amend or a reset, --keep for

167
00:12:41,366 --> 00:12:46,434
dropping and --merge ORIG_HEAD for a fresh
merge. Shared commits are corrected with

168
00:12:46,474 --> 00:12:49,695
a revert, and a reverted
merge needs a re-merge plan.

169
00:12:51,535 --> 00:12:58,943
Read sections 11.12 to 11.16 of Chapter
11 and do the Practice section 11.18.

170
00:12:59,283 --> 00:13:05,115
Do Exercise 8.1, Level 1, "take a
commit back and make it again", in

171
00:13:05,155 --> 00:13:07,965
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

172
00:13:08,305 --> 00:13:12,795
Seven times today you asked about
publication before you named a command.

173
00:13:12,835 --> 00:13:17,822
That's the reflex, and it's yours now.
Work through the scenario cards before the

174
00:13:17,862 --> 00:13:22,973
next video. Next time, a new module: what
a rebase is, and why every rebased commit

175
00:13:23,013 --> 00:13:27,069
has a new ID. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

176
00:13:27,109 --> 00:13:28,559
See you in the next one.
